  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="hw04-P6-6-0">
      <title>Problem 6.6.0: Conceptual Questions</title>

      <task xml:id="hw04-P6-6-0-a" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>What is the definition of scale equivariant?</p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw04-P6-6-0-b" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Why is it important to standardize the predictors when using
            ridge regression or the lasso?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw04-P6-6-0-c" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>What is the difference between ridge regression and the lasso?</p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw04-P6-6-0-d" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What is an advantage of using ridge regression or the lasso over
            least-squares linear regression?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw04-P6-6-0-e" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>What is the purpose of principal component analysis (PCA)?</p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw04-P6-6-0-f" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>What does the first principal component maximize?</p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="hw04-P6-6-9">
      <title>Problem 6.6.9: Predicting College Applications</title>

      <introduction>
        <!-- The source notebook's part (a) says to use the split only for
             least squares, but parts (c)-(f) explicitly request training-set
             fits and held-out test errors. Follow those specific tasks. -->
        <p>
          Predict the number of applications received using the other variables
          in the <c>College</c> data set. Use a single training/test split for
          all five methods below. Choose tuning parameters by cross-validation
          on the training set, then report error on the held-out test set. This
          makes the reported test errors comparable across methods.
        </p>

        <program language="python">
          <code>
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
import statsmodels.api as sm

from sklearn.cross_decomposition import PLSRegression
from sklearn.decomposition import PCA
from sklearn.linear_model import LassoCV, LinearRegression, RidgeCV
from sklearn.metrics import root_mean_squared_error
from sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV, KFold, train_test_split
from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler
          </code>
        </program>
<p>We will use the <xref ref="college-data"/>. Load the data into <c>college_df</c>, then run the following.</p>

        <program language="python">          
          <code>
college_df = college_df.set_index("Unnamed: 0")
college_df = pd.get_dummies(college_df, drop_first=True)
college_df = college_df.astype(float)
college_df.head()
          </code>
          </program>
          
          
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="hw04-P6-6-9-a">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Split the data into a training set and a test set. Separate
            <c>Apps</c> as the response and use the remaining variables as
            predictors.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw04-P6-6-9-b">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Fit a linear model using least squares on the training set, and
            report its test error.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw04-P6-6-9-c">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Fit a ridge regression model, choosing <m>\lambda</m> by
            cross-validation on the training set. Report its test error.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw04-P6-6-9-d">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Fit a lasso model, choosing <m>\lambda</m> by cross-validation
            on the training set. Report its test error and the number of
            nonzero coefficient estimates.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw04-P6-6-9-e">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Fit a principal components regression (PCR) model, choosing the
            number of components <m>M</m> by cross-validation on the training
            set. Report its test error and the selected value of <m>M</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw04-P6-6-9-f">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Fit a partial least squares (PLS) model, choosing the number of
            components <m>M</m> by cross-validation on the training set.
            Report its test error and the selected value of <m>M</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="hw04-P6-6-9-g" workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Comment on the results. How accurately can the number of college
            applications received be predicted? Is there much difference
            among the test errors from these five approaches?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
